      "examAnswer": "A pointer to pointer is a variable that stores the address of another pointer and is declared using two indirection operators, for example int **q; the value is obtained by double dereferencing as **q. An array of pointers is an array in which every element stores an address, such as char *names[3], which is memory efficient for strings of differing lengths but has the limitations that string literals cannot be modified, the strings are not stored contiguously, and dynamically allocated rows must each be freed individually. Command line arguments are values supplied to a program at the time of execution and are received by main(int argc, char *argv[]), where argc is the argument count including the program name and argv is an array of pointers to those argument strings, with argv[0] holding the program name and argv[argc] equal to NULL.",
