    {
      "title": "Pointers and Functions",
      "teacher": "Normally a function gets a photocopy of your data and cannot touch the original. But sometimes you WANT it to change the original — like a teacher correcting your actual answer sheet, not a photocopy of it. Pointers make that possible.",
      "explain": "Pointers combine with functions in three ways. Passing a pointer as a <strong>parameter</strong> gives call by reference, so the function can modify the caller's variable. A <strong>function returning a pointer</strong> hands back an address instead of a value. A <strong>pointer to function</strong> stores the address of a function itself, so you can decide at run time which function to call.",
      "keyPoints": [
        "<strong>Function as parameter (call by reference)</strong>: pass <code>&amp;x</code>, receive <code>int *a</code>, change with <code>*a</code>.",
        "Call by value copies the data; call by reference passes the address.",
        "Passing a pointer is also faster for big data — no copying of a whole structure.",
        "<strong>Function returning a pointer</strong>: declared as <code>int* biggest(...)</code>.",
        "<strong>Never return the address of a local variable</strong> — it dies when the function ends.",
        "It is safe to return a pointer to memory from <code>malloc()</code>, or to something the caller owns.",
        "<strong>Pointer to function</strong>: <code>int (*fp)(int, int) = add;</code> then call it as <code>fp(4, 3)</code>.",
        "The brackets matter: <code>int (*fp)(int,int)</code> is a function pointer, <code>int *fp(int,int)</code> is a function returning int*."
      ],
      "subtopics": [
        {
          "title": "Call by value vs call by reference",
          "text": "In <strong>call by value</strong> the function gets copies, so changes stay inside the function — the famous failed swap. In <strong>call by reference</strong> you pass addresses, so <code>*a</code> and <code>*b</code> reach the caller's actual variables and the swap works. C technically always passes by value; passing a pointer just means the <em>address</em> is what gets copied, which is enough to reach the original."
        },
        {
          "title": "The dangling return trap",
          "text": "<code>int* wrong() { int x = 10; return &amp;x; }</code> compiles, but <code>x</code> lives on the stack and is destroyed the moment the function returns. The caller gets an address to dead memory. Return a pointer only to <code>malloc()</code>'d memory, to a <code>static</code> variable, or to something the caller passed in."
        },
        {
          "title": "Why pointers to functions are useful",
          "text": "Because a function's name is also an address, you can store it, pass it and call it later. This is how one sorting routine can sort in any order — you hand it the comparison function. The standard library's <code>qsort()</code> works exactly this way. It lets you choose behaviour while the program runs, not while you write it."
        }
      ],
      "realLife": "Giving a shopkeeper your actual bank card (reference — your balance really changes) versus showing him a photocopy of a currency note (value — nothing real happens). And a function pointer is like a phone contact saved as 'Plumber' — you can change which number that contact points to without changing who you call.",
      "code": "#include <stdio.h>\n\nvoid swap(int *a, int *b) {      // pointer as parameter = call by reference\n    int t = *a;\n    *a = *b;\n    *b = t;\n}\n\nint* bigger(int *a, int *b) {    // function RETURNING a pointer\n    return (*a > *b) ? a : b;    // safe: these belong to the caller\n}\n\nint add(int a, int b) { return a + b; }\nint sub(int a, int b) { return a - b; }\n\nint main() {\n    int x = 5, y = 9;\n\n    swap(&x, &y);                       // pass ADDRESSES\n    printf(\"%d %d\\n\", x, y);           // 9 5 - originals really changed\n\n    int *big = bigger(&x, &y);\n    printf(\"bigger = %d\\n\", *big);     // 9\n\n    int (*fp)(int, int) = add;          // pointer to function\n    printf(\"%d\\n\", fp(4, 3));          // 7\n    fp = sub;                           // point it at another function\n    printf(\"%d\\n\", fp(4, 3));          // 1\n    return 0;\n}",
      "codeNote": "One program, three ideas: pointer parameters change originals, a function hands back an address, and fp calls whichever function it points to.",
      "mistake": "Returning the address of a local variable: <code>int* f(){ int x=5; return &amp;x; }</code>. The variable is destroyed when f() ends, so the caller holds a dangling pointer and gets garbage or a crash.",
      "examAnswer": "Pointers may be used with functions in three ways. When a pointer is passed as a parameter, the function receives the address of the caller's variable and can therefore modify the original value; this mechanism is known as call by reference and is commonly used to implement functions such as swap(). A function may also return a pointer, declared as int* fun(), but it must never return the address of a local variable because that variable is destroyed when the function terminates. A pointer to function stores the address of a function and is declared as int (*fp)(int, int); the function is then invoked through the pointer as fp(a, b), which allows the function to be selected during execution.",
      "remember": "&amp; in the call, * in the function = the original changes. (*fp) with brackets = pointer to function.",
      "why": "Call by reference is how a C function returns more than one result, and function pointers are the foundation of callbacks and menu-driven programs.",
      "practice": "Write a function that takes two addresses and returns both the sum and the product to the caller without using a return statement for either."
