      "realLife": "A college admission form. One form has your roll number (int), your name (string), your marks (floats) and your address (a smaller form inside the big form). 60 students means 60 forms in a file — that file is an array of structures.",
      "code": "#include <stdio.h>\n\nstruct Address {              // this will be nested inside Student\n    char city[20];\n    int  pin;\n};\n\nstruct Student {\n    int   roll;\n    char  name[30];\n    float marks[3];           // an ARRAY inside a structure\n    struct Address addr;      // a STRUCTURE inside a structure\n};\n\nint main() {\n    struct Student s1 = {21, \"Asha\", {88.0, 79.5, 91.0},\n                         {\"Bhopal\", 462001}};\n    struct Student cls[3];    // an ARRAY OF structures\n\n    printf(\"%d %s\\n\", s1.roll, s1.name);          // dot operator\n    printf(\"%.1f\\n\", s1.marks[1]);                // 79.5\n    printf(\"%s %d\\n\", s1.addr.city, s1.addr.pin); // two dots for nesting\n\n    cls[0] = s1;              // a whole structure copied in one line\n    printf(\"%s\\n\", cls[0].name);                  // Asha\n    printf(\"size = %zu bytes\\n\", sizeof(struct Student));\n    return 0;\n}",
