    {
      "title": "Pointer to Structure and Self-Referential Structures",
      "teacher": "Here is a beautiful, slightly strange idea: what if a structure contains a pointer to a structure of its very own type? That single trick is how a linked list, a stack, a queue and a tree are all built.",
      "explain": "A <strong>pointer to structure</strong> stores the address of a structure variable. With a normal variable you use the dot <code>.</code>, but with a pointer you use the <strong>arrow (referential) operator <code>-&gt;</code></strong>: <code>p-&gt;roll</code> is a cleaner way of writing <code>(*p).roll</code>. A <strong>self-referential structure</strong> is one that contains a pointer to its own type, which lets one record point to the next one — this is the basis of the <strong>linked list</strong>.",
      "keyPoints": [
        "<code>struct Student *p = &amp;s1;</code> — p holds the structure's address.",
