        {
          "title": "Why the arrow exists",
          "text": "To reach a member through a pointer you must first dereference, then select: <code>(*p).roll</code>. The brackets are needed because <code>.</code> binds tighter than <code>*</code>, so <code>*p.roll</code> would be read as <code>*(p.roll)</code> and fail. Writing those brackets everywhere is painful, so C provides <code>-&gt;</code> as a shortcut. The two forms are 100% identical in meaning."
        },
        {
          "title": "How self-reference builds a linked list",
          "text": "Each <code>struct Node</code> holds some data and a pointer <code>next</code> to another Node. Node A's next points to Node B, B's next points to C, and C's next is <code>NULL</code> to mark the end. Now you can grow the chain one node at a time with <code>malloc()</code>, with no fixed size and no shifting of elements — the thing an array can never do."
        },
        {
          "title": "Why it must be a pointer",
          "text": "If <code>struct Node</code> contained a full <code>struct Node next;</code> inside itself, its size would be its own size plus its own size, for ever — the compiler cannot compute that and rejects it. A <strong>pointer</strong> to Node, however, has a known fixed size, so the declaration is legal even though the type is not yet complete."
        }
