      "realLife": "A treasure hunt in college fest. Each clue slip holds a message AND tells you where the next slip is. The last slip says 'that's all' — that is NULL. Each slip is a node, and the chain of slips is a linked list.",
      "code": "#include <stdio.h>\n#include <stdlib.h>\n\nstruct Node {                 // SELF-REFERENTIAL structure\n    int data;\n    struct Node *next;        // a pointer to its OWN type\n};\n\nint main() {\n    struct Node a = {10, NULL};\n    struct Node *p = &a;      // pointer to structure\n\n    printf(\"%d\\n\", (*p).data);   // the long way\n    printf(\"%d\\n\", p->data);     // the arrow - same meaning, cleaner\n\n    // build a second node at run time and link it\n    struct Node *b = (struct Node *) malloc(sizeof(struct Node));\n    if (b == NULL) return 1;\n    b->data = 20;\n    b->next = NULL;           // NULL marks the end of the list\n    a.next  = b;              // 10 -> 20 : a tiny linked list\n\n    printf(\"%d %d\\n\", p->data, p->next->data);   // 10 20\n\n    free(b);                  // release what malloc gave\n    return 0;\n}",
      "codeNote": "p->next->data follows the chain one link at a time — that is a linked list traversal in miniature.",
