      "examAnswer": "A pointer to a structure stores the address of a structure variable and is declared as struct Student *p; the address is assigned as p = &s1. Members are accessed through such a pointer using the arrow or referential operator, so that p->roll is equivalent to (*p).roll, in which the parentheses are necessary because the dot operator has higher precedence than the indirection operator. A self-referential structure is a structure that contains a pointer to a structure of its own type, for example struct Node { int data; struct Node *next; };. Since the member is a pointer, its size is known and the declaration is valid. Self-referential structures, combined with dynamic memory allocation, form the basis of linked lists, stacks, queues and trees.",
      "remember": "Dot for a variable, arrow for a pointer. A node pointing to its own type = a linked list.",
      "why": "Data Structures next semester is built almost entirely on this one topic — linked lists, stacks, queues and trees are all self-referential structures plus malloc.",
