      "examAnswer": "A union is a user-defined data type whose members share the same memory location, and therefore the size of a union is equal to the size of its largest member, whereas a structure allocates separate memory for each member and its size is at least the sum of its members. Consequently, all members of a structure may hold valid values simultaneously, while in a union only the member written most recently holds a valid value. An enumerated data type, declared with the enum keyword, defines a set of named integer constants whose values begin at zero by default and increase by one unless assigned explicitly. The typedef keyword is used to provide an alternative name for an existing data type, which improves readability, for example typedef struct { int roll; } Student;.",
